  ('sephora-beauty-preferences-response', 'What Sephora''s New Hair Quiz Gets Wrong About Type 4 Hair', 'response-video', 'Sephora just launched hair personalization. Here are the 6 variables they left out.', '*Note: this post summarizes a video response and reflects our read of Sephora''s quiz at the time of recording. Quiz questions change — check the current version before treating specifics as gospel.*

## What Sephora is asking

Sephora''s new hair personalization quiz is a real step forward for an industry that has mostly treated "hair care" as one undifferentiated category. Broadly, it asks shoppers to self-report across categories like:

1. **Hair texture** (straight, wavy, curly, coily — a broad, simplified version of the 1-4 typing system)
2. **Length**
3. **Color-treated status**
4. **Scalp oiliness / wash frequency**
5. **Current styling goals** (volume, definition, smoothing, etc.)
6. **Top hair concerns** (frizz, breakage, dryness, etc.)
7. **Current product routine / what''s already in your cabinet**

That''s a genuinely useful starting point, and it will probably improve product recommendations for a lot of shoppers compared to no personalization at all. But it stops well short of the variables that actually drive whether a product works for type 4 hair specifically.

## The 6 variables missing from this picture

1. **Porosity.** Whether your cuticle is tightly bound (low) or raised/gapped (high) determines how your hair absorbs and holds moisture and product — arguably more than texture does. Two people with identical curl patterns can need opposite humectant strategies based on porosity alone.
2. **Scalp condition.** Dry, oily, sensitive, and dandruff-prone scalps each tolerate different actives and frequencies. "How oily is your scalp" gets at frequency, not at sensitivity or barrier health.
3. **Climate.** As we covered in our glycerin breakdown, the same humectant-heavy product can hydrate hair in a humid climate and dry it out in an arid one. A quiz with no climate input is recommending the same product to someone in Phoenix and someone in Houston.
4. **Water hardness.** Hard water mineral buildup changes how product performs and how often clarifying is needed — a meaningfully different routine need that has nothing to do with texture or color treatment.
5. **Strand thickness.** Fine, medium, and coarse strands carry product differently. A styling cream that''s perfect for coarse strands can weigh down fine ones regardless of curl pattern.
6. **The 4a/4b/4c distinction.** Lumping all coily hair into one "coily" bucket erases real differences in curl diameter, shrinkage, and moisture needs that this community already understands intuitively. A 4a and a 4c pattern can need substantially different product weights and techniques even with identical porosity.

## Why this gap matters more for type 4 hair than other textures

For straighter and looser-wave hair types, missing these six variables is a real but smaller gap — there''s less variance in how those textures respond to humectants, climate shifts, and product weight, so a texture-and-concern-based quiz still gets shoppers reasonably close to the right product.

Type 4 hair doesn''t have that margin for error. It spans the widest porosity range of any hair type, is the most climate-sensitive in terms of moisture balance, and carries the most variation in strand thickness and curl diameter within a single typing category. A quiz that treats "coily" as one bucket and skips porosity, climate, and scalp condition isn''t slightly imprecise for type 4 hair — it''s missing most of the variables that actually predict whether a recommended product will work.

This is exactly the gap we built this tool to close. Texture is a starting point, not the whole picture.
